1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,046
Earth's longest mountain range was built
without a single collision.

2
00:00:04,046 --> 00:00:08,686
A mid-ocean ridge forms where two tectonic
plates are pulled apart: the mantle

3
00:00:08,686 --> 00:00:13,386
beneath rises to fill the gap, melts as
its pressure drops, and erupts as fresh

4
00:00:13,386 --> 00:00:15,707
basalt that cools into new ocean crust.

5
00:00:15,707 --> 00:00:21,060
NOAA describes the result as a continuous
range of underwater volcanoes stretching

6
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:25,760
nearly 65,000 kilometres around the globe,
like the seams on a baseball.

7
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:28,581
That figure tells you how big the system
is.

8
00:00:28,581 --> 00:00:33,837
It does not tell you why rock rises, why
it melts without being heated, or why the

9
00:00:33,837 --> 00:00:35,440
ridge stands tall at all.

10
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:40,258
And it hides an older assumption that this
evidence overturned: that mountains are

11
00:00:40,258 --> 00:00:42,609
what you get when the crust is squeezed.

12
00:00:42,609 --> 00:00:45,312
This one is what you get when it is
stretched.

13
00:00:45,312 --> 00:00:47,675
How is a mid-ocean ridge formed?

14
00:00:47,675 --> 00:00:50,417
Start with what a mid-ocean ridge is not.

15
00:00:50,417 --> 00:00:55,569
The Alps and the Himalayas are folds,
crust crumpled and stacked where plates

16
00:00:55,569 --> 00:00:56,171
converge.

17
00:00:56,171 --> 00:01:01,523
A mid-ocean ridge is a crack being filled,
not a fold being pushed up.

18
00:01:01,523 --> 00:01:06,429
Along its crest, two plates move away from
each other, and the gap between them never

19
00:01:06,429 --> 00:01:07,064
stays open.

20
00:01:07,064 --> 00:01:10,321
Something from below always arrives to
close it.

21
00:01:10,321 --> 00:01:13,171
The ridge is the shape that filling takes.

22
00:01:13,171 --> 00:01:16,141
That changes how you should picture the
process.

23
00:01:16,141 --> 00:01:20,844
It is tempting to imagine molten rock
forcing its way upward and shoving the

24
00:01:20,844 --> 00:01:23,629
plates aside, like a wedge driven into a
log.

25
00:01:23,629 --> 00:01:29,232
The mainstream view, which you will find
in the plate tectonics material published

26
00:01:29,232 --> 00:01:31,624
by USGS, runs mostly the other way.

27
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:35,724
The plates are pulled apart by forces
acting elsewhere, chiefly the weight of

28
00:01:35,724 --> 00:01:39,505
old, cold plate sinking back into the
mantle at subduction zones, often

29
00:01:39,505 --> 00:01:41,581
thousands of kilometres from the ridge.

30
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:43,371
The ridge responds to that pull.

31
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:46,504
It is less an engine than an opening that
keeps healing.

32
00:01:46,504 --> 00:01:51,840
Here is the sequence, in short form:
plates pulled apart, mantle rises, rock

33
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:53,315
melts, basalt erupts.

34
00:01:53,315 --> 00:01:58,301
The mantle beneath the crest rises
passively into the space the plates leave

35
00:01:58,301 --> 00:02:01,241
As it rises, some of that rock melts.

36
00:02:01,241 --> 00:02:06,723
The melt, lighter than the solid rock
around it, collects and climbs.

37
00:02:06,723 --> 00:02:11,653
It erupts onto the seafloor or freezes
just beneath it, and the cooled basalt

38
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:16,200
becomes new ocean crust, welded onto the
trailing edges of both plates.

39
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:20,301
Then the plates keep moving, and the cycle
begins again on the same seam.

40
00:02:20,301 --> 00:02:23,618
The step that surprises most people is the
melting.

41
00:02:23,618 --> 00:02:26,739
The mantle beneath a ridge is mostly solid
rock.

42
00:02:26,739 --> 00:02:32,520
It is extremely hot, but it is also under
enormous pressure, and pressure raises the

43
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,723
temperature at which rock melts.

44
00:02:34,723 --> 00:02:37,567
Hold that rock deep down and it stays
solid.

45
00:02:37,567 --> 00:02:42,803
Let it rise, and the pressure on it falls
faster than the rock can lose its heat.

46
00:02:42,803 --> 00:02:47,459
At some point it crosses its own melting
point without any heat being added at all.

47
00:02:47,459 --> 00:02:52,550
Geologists call this decompression
melting: rock melting because the pressure

48
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,592
on it drops, not melting caused by added
heat.

49
00:02:55,592 --> 00:03:00,536
Nobody can watch this happen, so it is
inferred from three lines of evidence.

50
00:03:00,536 --> 00:03:05,154
First, lab experiments on mantle rock
squeeze and heat samples to map the

51
00:03:05,154 --> 00:03:07,432
conditions where they begin to melt.

52
00:03:07,432 --> 00:03:12,497
Second, the composition of ridge basalt,
dredged and drilled from the seafloor,

53
00:03:12,497 --> 00:03:16,280
matches what partial melting of mantle
rock should produce.

54
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:21,035
Only a fraction of the rock melts, and
that melt carries a recognisable chemical

55
00:03:21,035 --> 00:03:21,629
signature.

56
00:03:21,629 --> 00:03:26,652
Third, seismic images beneath ridges show
zones where sound waves slow down, which

57
00:03:26,652 --> 00:03:29,837
is what you would expect where some melt
is present.

58
00:03:29,837 --> 00:03:32,305
None of these alone proves the process.

59
00:03:32,305 --> 00:03:36,733
Together they point the same way: the
mantle partly melts as it rises.

60
00:03:36,733 --> 00:03:42,925
The ocean, the interior of the planet,
underground ecosystems, even parts of our

61
00:03:42,925 --> 00:03:47,491
own bodies. We live inside systems we
cannot fully explain.

62
00:03:47,491 --> 00:03:48,370
Now the height.

63
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:53,235
If nothing is being squeezed, it is fair
to wonder why there is a mountain range at

64
00:03:53,235 --> 00:03:53,469
all.

65
00:03:53,469 --> 00:03:55,107
The answer is temperature.

66
00:03:55,107 --> 00:04:00,272
Freshly made crust, and the mantle just
beneath it, are hot, and hot rock takes up

67
00:04:00,272 --> 00:04:02,413
more room than the same rock cold.

68
00:04:02,413 --> 00:04:04,508
So the newest seafloor rides high.

69
00:04:04,508 --> 00:04:09,007
As the plates carry it away from the
crest, it cools, contracts and grows

70
00:04:09,007 --> 00:04:12,211
denser, and it settles lower over millions
of years.

71
00:04:12,211 --> 00:04:17,340
The ridge is a long, broad swell of warm,
young rock, sloping down on either side

72
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,507
into the older, colder, deeper floor of
the abyss.

73
00:04:20,507 --> 00:04:25,323
Its height is hot rock taking up more
room, not rock piled up by force.

74
00:04:25,323 --> 00:04:28,361
That explanation has a consequence you can
test.

75
00:04:28,361 --> 00:04:33,363
If the height comes from cooling, then
seafloor depth should track seafloor age

76
00:04:33,363 --> 00:04:37,731
in a predictable way: shallow at the
crest, deeper with distance, and

77
00:04:37,731 --> 00:04:42,416
flattening out as the rock approaches the
temperature of its surroundings.

78
00:04:42,416 --> 00:04:46,493
Depth surveys across the major ocean
basins show broadly that pattern.

79
00:04:46,493 --> 00:04:51,212
It is one of the quieter confirmations
that the ridge is a thermal feature, not a

80
00:04:51,212 --> 00:04:52,085
structural one.

81
00:04:52,085 --> 00:04:54,022
The modern story begins with maps.

82
00:04:54,022 --> 00:04:58,352
In the years after the Second World War,
research ships crossed the Atlantic

83
00:04:58,352 --> 00:05:03,195
running echo sounders, timing sound pulses
to the seafloor and back to measure depth.

84
00:05:03,195 --> 00:05:08,384
Marie Tharp, working with the geologist
Bruce Heezen, turned those echo-sounding

85
00:05:08,384 --> 00:05:12,600
depth profiles into hand-drawn
cross-sections of the ocean floor.

86
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:17,811
Lined up side by side, the profiles showed
something consistent: a valley along the

87
00:05:17,811 --> 00:05:22,205
crest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, running
down its centre like a notch.

88
00:05:22,205 --> 00:05:26,157
Tharp argued it was a rift, a place where
the crust was splitting.

89
00:05:26,157 --> 00:05:30,878
Heezen was at first sceptical, because
continental drift was then a minority view

90
00:05:30,878 --> 00:05:31,869
among geologists.

91
00:05:31,869 --> 00:05:36,045
But the pattern held as more soundings
came in, and it lined up with where

92
00:05:36,045 --> 00:05:38,302
earthquakes clustered beneath the ocean.

93
00:05:38,302 --> 00:05:41,688
A rift where the crust splits was the
reading that survived.

94
00:05:41,688 --> 00:05:44,707
A map, though, shows shape. It cannot show
motion.

95
00:05:44,707 --> 00:05:49,416
A valley along a ridge is consistent with
spreading, but it does not prove it.

96
00:05:49,416 --> 00:05:53,944
For that, someone needed a way to read
time off the seafloor itself.

97
00:05:53,944 --> 00:05:55,674
The key came from magnetism.

98
00:05:55,674 --> 00:06:00,246
When basalt cools at the ridge,
iron-bearing minerals inside it align with

99
00:06:00,246 --> 00:06:05,374
Earth's magnetic field, and that alignment
is fixed as the rock drops below what is

100
00:06:05,374 --> 00:06:07,165
called the Curie temperature.

101
00:06:07,165 --> 00:06:11,267
The iron minerals lock in the field as it
was at that moment.

102
00:06:11,267 --> 00:06:13,913
And Earth's field does not stay put.

103
00:06:13,913 --> 00:06:19,792
Over geological time, the field reverses,
with magnetic north and south swapping

104
00:06:19,792 --> 00:06:20,307
places.

105
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:25,917
Ships towing magnetometers had already
found long stripes of seafloor magnetised

106
00:06:25,917 --> 00:06:28,301
alternately one way and the other.

107
00:06:28,301 --> 00:06:33,101
Fred Vine and Drummond Matthews, and
independently Lawrence Morley, proposed

108
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:38,089
that the stripes mirror on both sides of a
ridge because new crust forms at the

109
00:06:38,089 --> 00:06:41,878
centre and carries each reversal outward
in both directions.

110
00:06:41,878 --> 00:06:44,531
In other words, the seafloor is spreading.

111
00:06:44,531 --> 00:06:48,838
Vine and Matthews published their argument
in the journal Nature, and it turned an

112
00:06:48,838 --> 00:06:51,096
earlier hypothesis into something
testable.

113
00:06:51,096 --> 00:06:56,414
Harry Hess had already proposed seafloor
spreading: new crust forming at a ridge

114
00:06:56,414 --> 00:06:58,408
and moving away on both sides.

115
00:06:58,408 --> 00:07:03,470
The magnetic stripes were the record that
idea predicted, a symmetrical tape running

116
00:07:03,470 --> 00:07:04,856
outward from the crest.

117
00:07:04,856 --> 00:07:07,819
Notice what the idea did not explain on
its own.

118
00:07:07,819 --> 00:07:10,227
It does not say what drives the plates.

119
00:07:10,227 --> 00:07:13,658
That part came later, and it is still
being refined.

120
00:07:13,658 --> 00:07:18,936
That the seafloor spreads is one finding.
Why it spreads is a separate question.

121
00:07:18,936 --> 00:07:23,681
A good hypothesis sticks its neck out, and
spreading made predictions that could have

122
00:07:23,681 --> 00:07:24,072
failed.

123
00:07:24,072 --> 00:07:28,620
If the crust is born at the ridge, then
sediment thickens away from the ridge,

124
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:33,285
because older floor has had longer to
collect the particles that drift down from

125
00:07:33,285 --> 00:07:33,635
above.

126
00:07:33,635 --> 00:07:36,361
And the crust grows older away from the
ridge.

127
00:07:36,361 --> 00:07:41,044
The drilling ship Glomar Challenger,
working for the Deep Sea Drilling Project,

128
00:07:41,044 --> 00:07:45,608
recovered cores across the South Atlantic
and found exactly that progression.

129
00:07:45,608 --> 00:07:50,500
The oldest sediment lying directly on the
basalt got older with distance from the

130
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:51,768
crest, on both sides.

131
00:07:51,768 --> 00:07:55,901
The pattern fit. The question of what
drives the plates stayed open.

132
00:07:55,901 --> 00:07:57,639
There was another prediction.

133
00:07:57,639 --> 00:08:02,613
If seawater seeps into hot, freshly made
crust, it should be heated and pushed back

134
00:08:02,613 --> 00:08:05,069
out, with hot water venting at the crest.

135
00:08:05,069 --> 00:08:10,594
In 1977, scientists diving in the
submersible Alvin, operated by Woods Hole

136
00:08:10,594 --> 00:08:16,707
Oceanographic Institution, known as WHOI,
found hydrothermal vents on the Galápagos

137
00:08:16,707 --> 00:08:20,243
Rift, a spreading centre in the eastern
Pacific.

138
00:08:20,243 --> 00:08:21,728
They expected hot water.

139
00:08:21,728 --> 00:08:26,061
They did not expect the dense communities
of animals living around it.

140
00:08:26,061 --> 00:08:30,909
The geology was confirmed, and the biology
opened an entirely new field.

141
00:08:30,909 --> 00:08:35,140
Not every ridge looks like the one Tharp
drew. Speed matters.

142
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:40,134
USGS describes spreading at the
Mid-Atlantic Ridge as slow, on the order

143
00:08:40,134 --> 00:08:45,475
of a few centimetres a year, roughly the
pace at which your fingernails grow.

144
00:08:45,475 --> 00:08:50,105
Ridges that spread faster, such as the
East Pacific Rise, receive magma more

145
00:08:50,105 --> 00:08:50,653
steadily.

146
00:08:50,653 --> 00:08:55,887
A slow-spreading ridge tends to show a
deep central rift valley, not a smooth

147
00:08:55,887 --> 00:08:56,771
central high.

148
00:08:56,771 --> 00:09:01,853
Fast ridges show the reverse: a broad
swell with little or no valley at its

149
00:09:01,853 --> 00:09:06,626
Exact rates differ along each ridge and
between studies, so treat any single

150
00:09:06,626 --> 00:09:09,955
figure as an average over a long stretch
of seafloor.

151
00:09:09,955 --> 00:09:13,281
Fractures also break the ridge into offset
segments.

152
00:09:13,281 --> 00:09:18,399
These transform faults, where the plates
slide past each other sideways, are why

153
00:09:18,399 --> 00:09:22,493
the ridge zigzags on a map instead of
running in one clean line.

154
00:09:22,493 --> 00:09:25,692
And in one place the ridge rises above the
waves.

155
00:09:25,692 --> 00:09:30,979
Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge,
where an unusually generous magma supply,

156
00:09:30,979 --> 00:09:36,135
usually attributed to a mantle hotspot,
has built the crest high enough to form

157
00:09:36,135 --> 00:09:36,461
land.

158
00:09:36,461 --> 00:09:41,401
There you can stand inside a rift valley
in open air and see the process the deep

159
00:09:41,401 --> 00:09:42,560
sea normally hides.

160
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:46,971
As you watch, notice which claims carry a
number and which do not.

161
00:09:46,971 --> 00:09:49,958
The length of the ridge system comes from
NOAA.

162
00:09:49,958 --> 00:09:53,771
The description of spreading and plate
motion leans on USGS.

163
00:09:53,771 --> 00:10:00,499
The vent discovery is credited to Woods
Hole Oceanographic Institution, WHOI, and

164
00:10:00,499 --> 00:10:00,997
Alvin.

165
00:10:00,997 --> 00:10:06,442
Most of this story is told in mechanisms
rather than figures, because the mechanism

166
00:10:06,442 --> 00:10:09,067
is what the evidence actually pins down.

167
00:10:09,067 --> 00:10:10,543
Here is the edge of it.

168
00:10:10,543 --> 00:10:15,808
The evidence firmly establishes that new
crust forms at ridges, and that the ridge

169
00:10:15,808 --> 00:10:17,477
is high because it is hot.

170
00:10:17,477 --> 00:10:22,591
It does not establish exactly how melt
gathers beneath the crest: how it migrates

171
00:10:22,591 --> 00:10:27,515
upward through mostly solid rock and
focuses into a narrow band at the axis is

172
00:10:27,515 --> 00:10:29,472
still modelled and argued over.

173
00:10:29,472 --> 00:10:33,346
Nor does it settle how much each force
drives the plates.

174
00:10:33,346 --> 00:10:38,512
Slab pull is widely judged the largest,
but the balance between it and other

175
00:10:38,512 --> 00:10:41,435
forces is estimated, not measured
directly.

176
00:10:41,435 --> 00:10:46,228
Most of the ridge has never been seen up
close, and the mantle beneath it has never

177
00:10:46,228 --> 00:10:47,499
been sampled in place.

178
00:10:47,499 --> 00:10:49,490
So ocean ridges are not pushed up.

179
00:10:49,490 --> 00:10:54,467
The longest mountain range on Earth is not
a wall raised by collision but a seam that

180
00:10:54,467 --> 00:10:59,093
never finishes closing, built from rock
that melted because the weight above it

181
00:10:59,093 --> 00:11:01,728
lifted, and that will sink again as it
cools.

182
00:11:01,728 --> 00:11:06,747
Every stretch of ocean floor began there,
at a crest, as something briefly molten.
